::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which choice best defines academic language?
{
~A. Casual language used only with close friends#This describes informal language, not academic language.
~B. Language that uses the longest words possible#This is a misconception. Academic language should be precise, not unnecessarily complicated.
=C. Clear, precise language used for learning, explaining, and discussing ideas#Correct. Academic language helps speakers communicate ideas clearly in school settings.
~D. Language that avoids explaining ideas#Academic language should support explanation, not replace it.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word is most precise in a speech communication discussion?
{
=A. transition#Correct. Transition is precise vocabulary for a connection between parts of a speech.
~B. thing#This is also vague and forces the listener to guess.
~C. stuff#This is vague and does not identify the idea.
~D. whatever#This is informal and unclear.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence gives the clearest academic feedback?
{
~A. Your speech was pretty good.#This is positive but vague. It does not explain what worked.
~B. It sounded fine to me.#This does not give useful information for revision.
~C. I liked the thing at the start.#This is too vague because "thing" does not name the speech element.
=D. Your opening question connected the topic to the audience.#Correct. This names a speech part and explains how it helped communication.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Why should a speaker replace vague words with precise vocabulary?
{
~A. To make the message longer#Length is not the goal. Precision helps meaning.
=B. To help the audience understand the exact idea#Correct. Precise words make the speaker's meaning clearer.
~C. To avoid giving evidence#Precise vocabulary does not replace evidence. It helps explain evidence clearly.
~D. To make the audience feel confused#This is the opposite of the purpose. Precise vocabulary should reduce confusion.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response gives collaborative academic feedback?
{
=A. Your evidence is useful; we can add a transition so the audience follows the next point.#Correct. This response is respectful, uses academic language, and suggests a shared improvement.
~B. Your part is confusing, and I am done helping.#This is not collaborative and does not give a useful next step.
~C. I will redo your whole section myself.#This removes collaboration and does not help the speaker learn.
~D. Everything is wrong.#This is vague and discouraging.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which academic word names information used to support a claim?
{
~A. stuff#This is vague and not academic.
~B. thing#This is vague and does not name the academic role of the information.
=C. evidence#Correct. Evidence is information that supports a claim or explanation.
~D. noise#Noise is not the term for support used in a speech.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which feedback best identifies a specific speech section?
{
~A. It got weird somewhere.#This is vague and does not identify the section.
=B. The conclusion needs to restate the main claim more clearly.#Correct. This names the conclusion and explains the specific revision needed.
~C. It was not good in the middle part thing.#This is informal and unclear.
~D. Something in the speech was off.#This does not tell the speaker where to revise.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which phrase is most academic and precise?
{
~A. a bunch of talking#This is casual and vague.
~B. the speech thing#This does not name the idea clearly.
~C. some words#This is vague and not helpful.
=D. oral presentation#Correct. Oral presentation is precise academic vocabulary for a spoken presentation.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which response best explains how evidence helps an audience?
{
~A. Evidence lets the speaker avoid explaining the claim.#Evidence still needs explanation so the audience understands its connection.
~B. Evidence makes the speech longer for no reason.#This misunderstands evidence. Evidence should support the claim.
=C. Evidence gives the audience support for the speaker's claim.#Correct. This explains the role evidence plays in oral communication.
~D. Evidence is the same thing as tone.#Evidence supports ideas; tone communicates attitude.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response keeps feedback respectful and academic?
{
=A. Your main claim is clear, but the evidence needs more explanation.#Correct. This response is respectful, specific, and academically useful.
~B. Your speech is bad.#This is vague and disrespectful.
~C. I do not like your speaking.#This focuses on opinion and does not provide a clear improvement.
~D. You should start over because it is all wrong.#This is harsh and not specific.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence best replaces "The start was good"?
{
~A. The start had things.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
=B. The introduction clearly previewed the main points.#Correct. This uses precise vocabulary and explains what worked.
~C. The beginning was okay and stuff.#This remains vague and informal.
~D. The start was not bad.#This does not explain the strength.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
What should academic feedback include?
{
~A. A personal insult#Insults do not support communication or learning.
~B. Only a short opinion#An opinion alone is usually too vague to guide improvement.
=C. A specific communication skill or speech part#Correct. Academic feedback should identify what worked or what needs revision.
~D. No explanation#Without explanation, the speaker may not know what to improve.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word best names the speaker's main position?
{
~A. thing#This is vague and does not identify the role of the idea.
~B. stuff#This is not precise academic vocabulary.
~C. maybe#This does not name a speech element.
=D. claim#Correct. A claim is the speaker's main idea or position.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence best explains a transition?
{
=A. It is a word or phrase that connects one idea to the next.#Correct. A transition helps listeners follow the organization of the speech.
~B. It is a random word that fills time.#This misunderstands transitions. They connect ideas.
~C. It is the speaker's final sentence only.#A transition can happen between many parts of a speech, not only at the end.
~D. It is an unrelated example.#An unrelated example does not connect ideas.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best supports a classmate during revision?
{
~A. I cannot explain what is wrong, but change it.#This is not helpful because it gives no specific guidance.
=B. Your reason is clear; adding evidence would make it stronger for the audience.#Correct. This feedback is respectful, academic, and useful.
~C. Your part is worse than mine.#This compares people instead of improving the speech.
~D. The audience will hate this.#This is harsh and unsupported.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence uses academic language to explain audience impact?
{
~A. The audience gets the thing.#This answer does not fully meet the lesson standard. Recheck the audience, purpose, evidence, or communication skill before choosing.
~B. People know stuff after hearing it.#This is not precise.
~C. It works because it works.#This does not explain the audience impact.
=D. The example helps the audience understand the speaker's claim.#Correct. This sentence uses precise academic vocabulary and explains the audience benefit.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which pair is the best vague-to-academic revision?
{
~A. transition to whatever#This replaces academic vocabulary with unclear language.
~B. claim to thing#This moves from precise to vague, which weakens communication.
=C. stuff to evidence#Correct. Evidence is a precise academic replacement when discussing support for a claim.
~D. audience to people or something#This weakens precision because audience is the correct communication term.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which feedback best helps a speaker revise?
{
=A. Add a transition after the first reason so the audience can follow the second reason.#Correct. This names the action, location, and audience benefit.
~B. Make the whole thing better.#This is too vague to guide revision.
~C. It is confusing, but I do not know why.#This identifies confusion but does not provide enough guidance.
~D. Talk smarter next time.#This is vague and unhelpful.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response best combines collaboration and academic language?
{
~A. I will not use your evidence.#This rejects the idea without discussion.
=B. Your evidence supports the claim; let's decide where it fits best in the speech.#Correct. This response uses academic vocabulary and invites shared planning.
~C. Your idea is wrong.#This is vague and not collaborative.
~D. We should stop revising.#This avoids collaboration.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Why is "Your speech was good" weak feedback?
{
~A. It is too specific.#It is not specific enough. The speaker does not know what was good.
~B. It uses too much evidence.#It does not mention evidence at all.
~C. It is too academic for a classmate.#The problem is not that it is academic; the problem is that it is vague.
=D. It does not identify a specific strength or communication skill.#Correct. Useful academic feedback names what worked and why.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which academic vocabulary word names the people listening to a speech?
{
=A. audience#Correct. Audience is the communication term for the listeners or viewers receiving the message.
~B. stuff#This is vague and does not name the listeners.
~C. evidence#Evidence supports a claim; it does not name the listeners.
~D. transition#A transition connects ideas; it does not name the listeners.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence is clearest for explaining a revision?
{
~A. Change the thing by the other thing.#This is too vague.
~B. Make it sound more school-like.#This is vague and does not name the needed revision.
=C. Add evidence after the claim so the audience understands your reason.#Correct. This identifies what to add, where to add it, and why it helps.
~D. Put nicer words in it.#This does not explain the communication problem.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which feedback is most respectful and useful?
{
~A. I have no idea what you were trying to say.#This may sound dismissive and does not give a clear next step.
=B. Your topic is clear; now add a reason that explains why it matters to the audience.#Correct. This response gives a specific strength and a useful revision step.
~C. That was confusing, so start over.#This is too broad and discouraging.
~D. Nobody will understand you.#This is harsh and does not explain how to improve.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which statement best summarizes academic language in speaking?
{
~A. It means using words the audience cannot understand.#This is incorrect. Academic language should support understanding.
~B. It means making every sentence longer.#Length is not the goal.
~C. It means avoiding all explanation.#Academic language should help explanation.
=D. It means using precise words that make ideas clear.#Correct. Academic language helps speakers communicate exact meaning.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which response best completes this academic feedback: "Your claim is clear, but..."
{
=A. the evidence needs more explanation so the audience understands the connection.#Correct. This feedback uses academic language and explains how to improve.
~B. the thing should be better somehow.#This is vague and does not help the speaker revise.
~C. I did not like it much.#This is an opinion without academic explanation.
~D. you should just talk differently.#This is too broad to guide improvement.
}
